r cases in the pipeline that would  bring Chevron into into a question yes  this one there is one right now the  court took a case called Chi so reverses  and Wilke which involves deference to an  administrative agency's interpretation  of its own regulations that's called our  deference aue our deference I think I  see the lawyers nodding in here but the  in our deference the Court has always  held that administrative agencies can  interpret their own regulations any way  they want  once it's their regulation they can do  anything they want but the court has now  beginning to recognize that the  administrative agency can do a  tremendous amount of damage by changing  the interpretation of its regulations as  it did in this particular case kaizo  versus Wilkie so they have said they  will now review our deference a yu-er  deference if they decide as I believe  they will that our deference is no  longer going to be the the position of  the courts it will be an immediate  signal that they are going now to look  at Chevron and there are lots and lots  of cases involving Chevron every every  term of the court has at least one case  where Chevron can be introduced and  discussed so I don't think there'll be  any difficulty in getting a case before  the court and I think we will ultimately  have a decision in that direction but  again I want to say this it's very  important that the court understand that  there is support for the position that  they take if they are going to modify  Chevron because there will be huge  blowback from the left of the kind I'd  read to you and so conservatives have to  get out there and start talking about  the fact that it's important that these  decisions be made so that the Supreme  Court believes that the American people  are actually or at least a substantial  portion of the American people are with  them on this and we have to start doing  that before the decisions come down  because the decisions may not come down  the way we want them