e time in  court cases and the courts have over the  years pronounced what these things how  you define these things these things  it's called a jurisprudence so there  should be a jurisprudence of non  delegation where the court looks or all  the courts but certainly the supreme  court looks at a case that has come  before them and they say this is a case  where legislative authority has been  delegated that establishes a mark and  then there will be more marks and more  marks until it becomes pretty clear just  like it is in search and seizure when a  delegation has occurred I might say that  there is a case now before the Supreme  Court that was argued on October 2 we  have no decision yet could come any day  could come tomorrow as a matter of fact  but that decision the the Gundy case is  a very very narrow little case where the  question is whether someone is subject  to  a law that was passed at the state level  but also then later at the federal level  and the Congress did not say whether  people who had been convicted under the  law at the state level are also subject  to the federal law of disclosure and so  forth about their crime and what  Congress did was they gave it to the  Attorney General and they said you  decide now is that a delegation of  legislative authority shouldn't Congress  have made that decision how can they  hand it to the Attorney General that was  argued before the Supreme Court on  October 2 we'll have a decision but it's  so narrow that it might be exactly the  place where the court should be starting  they should start with something really  small one person who is who is who felt  he was Mis mishandled by a delegation of  legislative authority but once you do  that you're establishing a principle and  that principle then gets elucidated and  and and prayed over and and discussed  until you have a jurisprudence and  that's where we should be in ten or  fifteen years I fail to fully understand  the logic of the blowback argument that